======================================================================================================================
ITEM 31b -- is the almost-dark BTFR deficit REAL, or did h31 manufacture it?
======================================================================================================================
  Leisman+2017 HUDs: N = 115, of which 115 are matched back into alpha.100 for the flux and rms that set ALFALFA's own detection limit; 112 have a usable inclination and width
  h31's raw measurement reproduced, canonical : median log(v_obs/v_pred) = -0.296 +- 0.028, scatter 0.263 dex
  h31's raw measurement reproduced, alt       : median log(v_obs/v_pred) = -0.316 +- 0.028, scatter 0.263 dex
  [PASS] 31b.0 h31's measurement is reproduced exactly here from the same catalogues, so everything below is an audit of that number and not of a different one   (reproduced -0.296 dex against h31's -0.296)
  [PASS] 31b.0b the alpha.100 signal-to-noise definition is reproduced from the published columns, which is what licenses the width-dependent detection limit W_max = (1000 F/(6.5 rms))^2/20 used throughout section 4   (median recomputed/catalogue SNR = 1.0034, N = 31502)

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. TEST ONE -- the geometry bound: can ANY disc shape rescue the framework?
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  For an oblate spheroid of intrinsic axis ratio q0, b/a = sqrt((1-q0^2) cos^2 i + q0^2) >= cos i for every q0.
  So the measured axis ratio sets an ASSUMPTION-FREE floor on the inclination, i >= arccos(b/a), attained only in
  the razor-thin limit q0 -> 0.  A thicker disc always implies a MORE edge-on galaxy, hence a SMALLER v_obs.
  canonical : with EVERY galaxy given the most face-on inclination its own axis ratio permits (median 52 deg instead of 53 deg), the residual is still -0.287 +- 0.027 dex
  alt       : with EVERY galaxy given the most face-on inclination its own axis ratio permits (median 52 deg instead of 53 deg), the residual is still -0.307 +- 0.028 dex
  put the other way: for the framework to be right the median HUD would need an axis ratio of 0.921; SDSS measures 0.620
  and for 11 of the 112 galaxies the axis ratio the framework needs is SMALLER than the one measured, i.e. NO oblate geometry of any thickness can reconcile them -- the object would have to be rounder than it is observed to be
  [PASS] 31b.1 (this one survives the audit, and it is the strongest thing in item 31) the deficit is NOT a disc-thickness assumption.  Hubble's q0 = 0.2 is not doing the work: granting every galaxy the most face-on inclination its own measured axis ratio allows, for any oblate shape whatsoever, still leaves the almost-darks about a fifth of a dex below the framework's zero-parameter prediction.  This check asserts that the bound BITES, so it fails if the geometry escape works   (most-face-on-possible residual: canonical -0.287 +- 0.027, alt -0.307 +- 0.028 dex; 11 galaxies are geometrically irreconcilable)

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. TEST TWO -- the published counter-claim's mechanism: is the deficit a LOW-INCLINATION artefact?
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Sanchez Almeida 2024 (arXiv:2408.05269) attributes the UDG BTFR offset to systematics at low inclination.
  If that is the mechanism, the deficit MUST be concentrated in the face-on half of the sample, because v = W/(2 sin i)
  is the only place an inclination error can enter and its leverage grows as 1/sin i.
    measured inclination  0-40 deg (N =  27): median residual -0.106 +- 0.049 dex
    measured inclination 40-50 deg (N =  22): median residual -0.396 +- 0.091 dex
    measured inclination 50-60 deg (N =  33): median residual -0.344 +- 0.043 dex
    measured inclination 60-90 deg (N =  30): median residual -0.312 +- 0.038 dex
    face-on half (i < 50 deg, N = 49): -0.182 +- 0.059;  edge-on half (i >= 50 deg, N = 63): -0.325 +- 0.028
    Spearman(residual, inclination) = -0.292, p = 0.002
  [PASS] 31b.2 the low-inclination mechanism is NOT where this deficit lives.  The edge-on half of the sample -- where v = W/(2 sin i) is best conditioned and an inclination error has the least leverage -- carries a deficit at least as large as the face-on half.  That is the opposite of the published counter-claim's signature.  This check asserts the deficit is not carried by the face-on objects, so it FAILS if the counter-claim's mechanism is what is operating   (face-on half -0.182 +- 0.059 vs edge-on half -0.325 +- 0.028 dex (difference -0.143); trend with inclination p = 0.002)

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. TEST THREE -- can NOISE on the axis ratio manufacture a deficit?  (it cannot, and here is why)
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  log v = log W_rot - log(2 sin i).  -log sin i is a CONVEX function of i, so by Jensen's inequality symmetric
  scatter in the measured inclination raises the MEAN residual.  Random error makes galaxies look FASTER, not
  slower.  Only a systematic -- inclinations biased HIGH -- can push them down.  Mocked here rather than argued.
    Gaussian axis-ratio noise sigma(b/a) = 0.03: it moves the MEDIAN residual by +0.0001 dex (+ = looks faster)
    Gaussian axis-ratio noise sigma(b/a) = 0.05: it moves the MEDIAN residual by +0.0001 dex (+ = looks faster)
    Gaussian axis-ratio noise sigma(b/a) = 0.10: it moves the MEDIAN residual by +0.0003 dex (+ = looks faster)
  [PASS] 31b.3 noise cannot be the explanation, and the sign is a theorem, not a fit: symmetric error on the measured axis ratio biases the inferred rotation speed UPWARD, so it eats into the deficit rather than creating it.  Manufacturing a -0.30 dex deficit requires a SYSTEMATIC overestimate of every inclination -- sin i too large by a factor 2, the median galaxy actually at 23 deg where SDSS measures 52 deg -- not scatter   (5% axis-ratio noise shifts the median by +0.0001 dex (non-negative, as Jensen requires); the deficit needs sin i smaller by a factor 1.98)

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. TEST FOUR -- THE MECHANISM h31 MISSED, AND IT IS REAL: ALFALFA's detection limit depends on the line width
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  SNR = 1000 (F/W50) sqrt(W50/20) / rms  ->  SNR ~ F W50^(-1/2).  At fixed HI mass and distance a galaxy with a
  wider line has a LOWER signal-to-noise.  Detection therefore requires W50 <= W_max = (1000 F/(6.5 rms))^2/20,
  and at fixed baryonic mass and inclination that is a cut ON THE BTFR RESIDUAL: fast rotators are thrown away.
  median HUD: W50 observed 55 km/s, detection ceiling W_max = 100 km/s, and the width the SAME galaxy would have if it sat exactly ON the framework's BTFR is 100 km/s
    -> 50% of these galaxies, placed exactly on the framework's BTFR at their own HI mass, distance and inclination, would NOT have been detected by ALFALFA at all
  mass-matched control: N = 2898, raw median residual -0.031 dex; median SNR 9.4 against the HUDs' 8.2, median distance 65 Mpc against 79 Mpc

  what the width-selection alone manufactures, if the population is EXACTLY on the framework's BTFR:
    intrinsic scatter 0.15 dex: HUDs -0.043 dex (detected fraction 0.52), control -0.022 dex (detected fraction 0.81)  ->  manufactured DIFFERENTIAL -0.021 dex
    intrinsic scatter 0.20 dex: HUDs -0.074 dex (detected fraction 0.53), control -0.038 dex (detected fraction 0.79)  ->  manufactured DIFFERENTIAL -0.036 dex
    intrinsic scatter 0.26 dex: HUDs -0.117 dex (detected fraction 0.53), control -0.061 dex (detected fraction 0.77)  ->  manufactured DIFFERENTIAL -0.057 dex
    intrinsic scatter 0.35 dex: HUDs -0.183 dex (detected fraction 0.53), control -0.101 dex (detected fraction 0.75)  ->  manufactured DIFFERENTIAL -0.083 dex

  and therefore, de-selected: the INTRINSIC offset the almost-darks must have for the observed one to come out
    intrinsic scatter 0.15 dex -> intrinsic offset -0.273 dex (mock recovers a scatter of 0.143 against the observed 0.263)
    intrinsic scatter 0.20 dex -> intrinsic offset -0.254 dex (mock recovers a scatter of 0.186 against the observed 0.263)
    intrinsic scatter 0.26 dex -> intrinsic offset -0.223 dex (mock recovers a scatter of 0.234 against the observed 0.263)
    intrinsic scatter 0.35 dex -> intrinsic offset -0.159 dex (mock recovers a scatter of 0.298 against the observed 0.263)
    SELF-CONSISTENT: the intrinsic scatter that reproduces the observed 0.263 dex spread is 0.30 dex, and it implies an intrinsic offset of -0.194 dex
    (this is generous to the framework: the observed spread also contains inclination and mass errors, which the mock does not have, so the true intrinsic scatter is SMALLER and the true offset therefore MORE negative than -0.194)
  [PASS] 31b.4 AGAINST h31 AND AGAINST INTEREST: h31's headline number is inflated, and its 9.5 sigma is not a real error bar.  ALFALFA cannot detect a wide line as easily as a narrow one, so at fixed HI mass it preferentially keeps the SLOW rotators -- and about half of these galaxies would be undetectable if they sat on the framework's BTFR.  Modelling that selection removes roughly a third of the deficit.  This check asserts that the manufactured bias is a substantial part of h31's number, so it fails if the selection turns out to be harmless   (at the observed scatter the selection alone manufactures -0.117 dex in the HUDs against -0.061 in the control (-0.057 differential); 50% of BTFR-compliant HUDs would be undetected; de-selected offset -0.194 dex against the raw -0.296)

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4b. AND NOW AGAINST THE AUDIT'S OWN MODEL: the width cut predicts a trend with inclination.  It is too small.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  A wider line is harder to detect, and width scales as sin i, so the truncation must bite HARDER at high
  inclination.  Section 2 found exactly such a trend.  That is a quantitative prediction, so it can be checked -- and
  when it is, neither the selection model NOR a genuine constant offset reproduces the size of the observed trend.
    inclination bin   observed    if ON the BTFR (selection only)   if genuinely low
       0-40 deg (N = 27)    -0.106              -0.092                -0.247
      40-50 deg (N = 22)    -0.396              -0.168                -0.301
      50-60 deg (N = 33)    -0.344              -0.180                -0.325
      60-90 deg (N = 30)    -0.312              -0.164                -0.302
    observed face-on-to-edge-on span -0.205 dex; selection alone predicts -0.073, a genuine constant offset predicts -0.055, and 5% axis-ratio noise contributes +0.0009 in the face-on bin
  [PASS] 31b.4b AGAINST THE AUDIT'S OWN MODEL: the residual's trend with inclination is about three times steeper than the width selection can produce and steeper than any constant offset, and it is not axis-ratio noise.  Something inclination-dependent in these measurements is not modelled by h31, by the published counter-claim, or by this audit.  Consequently the sample MEDIAN is not a clean measurement of anything and item 31 cannot be quoted as a number to better than about 0.1 dex.  This check asserts that the models FAIL, so it passes only while they do   (observed span -0.205 dex vs selection -0.073 and constant-offset -0.055; axis-ratio noise contributes +0.0009)

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5. TEST FIVE -- the inclination distribution decides, and it uses no residual at all
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  The width cut of section 4 removes WIDE lines, and width scales as sin i.  So if the deficit were an artefact of
  that selection acting on a BTFR-compliant population, the surviving sample would have to be strongly FACE-ON
  biased.  If instead these galaxies are genuinely slow, their lines are narrow at every inclination and nothing is
  preferentially removed, so the sample stays randomly oriented.  The observed axis ratios settle it.
    OBSERVED: f(i > 45 deg) = 0.705 +- 0.043 (N = 112);  random orientation gives cos 45 deg = 0.707
    predicted f(i > 45) if the almost-darks sit ON the BTFR and selection makes the deficit: 0.606 (+2.3 sigma from the observed value)
    predicted f(i > 45) if the almost-darks are genuinely -0.19 dex low:                0.643 (+1.4 sigma from the observed value)
  [PASS] 31b.5 the inclination distribution -- which never touches the rotation speed -- favours a GENUINE deficit over a pure selection artefact.  A width cut severe enough to fake -0.30 dex would leave a visibly face-on sample; the almost-darks are randomly oriented to within the errors, which is what happens when the lines are narrow because the galaxies really are slow.  Underpowered on its own at N = 112, and reported as such   (observed 0.705 +- 0.043; on-the-BTFR-plus-selection predicts 0.606 (2.3 sigma away), genuinely-low predicts 0.643 (1.4 sigma away))

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
6. TEST SIX -- a control matched on DETECTABILITY, which is what h31's control was missing
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  ATTEMPT ONE, and it is a BUG IN MY OWN ESTIMATOR, reported rather than quietly dropped:
    matching the control to the HUDs on baryonic mass, SNR and distance gives a control median of -0.278 +- 0.016 dex and a differential of -0.017 dex -- apparently killing the whole effect.  It is not real.  SNR = 1000 (F/W50) sqrt(W50/20)/rms CONTAINS the line width, so matching on it conditions on the numerator of the very estimator being tested: a collider.
    the fingerprint is unmistakable: the SNR-matched control has a median W50 of 61 km/s where the mass-matched control has 113 km/s. Matching on SNR at fixed mass and distance can only be satisfied by picking NARROW lines, i.e. by selecting negative BTFR residuals directly.

  ATTEMPT TWO, the honest one: match on W_max = (1000 F/(6.5 rms))^2/20, the detection CEILING.  It is built from
  the integrated flux and the noise only -- no line width anywhere in it -- so it is a common cause and not a
  collider.  Matching baryonic mass, W_max and inclination matches the truncation exactly, because those three are
  what the truncation depends on.
  W_max-matched control (371 unique galaxies): median log W_max 2.01 vs the HUDs' 2.00; inclination 52 vs 53 deg; log M_b 9.14 vs 9.14; log M_HI 8.86 vs 8.96
    canonical : matched control -0.207 +- 0.018 (h31's mass-only control gave -0.031); DIFFERENTIAL = -0.089 +- 0.033 dex (-2.7 sigma)
    alt       : matched control -0.227 +- 0.019 (h31's mass-only control gave -0.051); DIFFERENTIAL = -0.089 +- 0.034 dex (-2.6 sigma)
  and the ladder that makes the mechanism plain: ordinary ALFALFA galaxies at ALL masses sit at +0.010 dex, the mass-matched control at -0.031, the W_max-matched control at -0.207, the almost-darks at -0.296.  The residual tracks DETECTABILITY, not the almost-dark property.
  [PASS] 31b.6 (this is the finding that resizes item 31) once the control is matched on detectability WITHOUT conditioning on the line width, ordinary ALFALFA galaxies of the same baryonic mass, HI mass, inclination and detection ceiling sit almost exactly where the almost-darks sit.  The almost-dark property itself is worth about a tenth of a dex, not the quarter-dex h31 reported: h31's control was nearer and easier to detect, so it was not bitten by the same width selection.  This check asserts that the differential is SMALL, so it fails if h31's -0.265 dex was right   (differential against the W_max-matched control -0.089 +- 0.033 dex, against h31's -0.265 dex; alt footing -0.089 dex)

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
7. TEST SEVEN -- the one object with resolved kinematics, run end to end
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  AGC 114905: ALFALFA W50 = 27 km/s, SDSS b/a = 0.77 -> this pipeline's inclination 41 deg -> pipeline rotation speed 13.9 km/s
    interferometry (Mancera Pina+2022) gives V_c = 23 km/s at i = 32 +- 3 deg, so the pipeline underestimates this galaxy's rotation speed by +0.218 dex
    the turbulence subtraction is most of it: sqrt(27^2 - 20^2) = 18.1 km/s against 2 V_c sin i = 24.4 km/s
    canonical : framework predicts 69.9 km/s; the resolved value leaves this object -0.483 dex low, and at the re-estimated i = 15 deg (47 km/s) -0.171 dex low
    alt       : framework predicts 73.2 km/s; the resolved value leaves this object -0.503 dex low, and at the re-estimated i = 15 deg (47 km/s) -0.192 dex low
  [PASS] 31b.7 AGAINST INTEREST: on the single object where the pipeline can be checked against interferometry it UNDERSTATES the rotation speed, so some of the sample deficit is the estimator's.  Applying that one object's end-to-end correction to the whole sample would move the deficit from -0.30 to about -0.09 dex -- but it is one galaxy, it is the narrowest-lined object in the sample where the turbulence subtraction is worst, and it cannot carry the sample.  Recorded as the largest single uncertainty in item 31   (pipeline 13.9 km/s vs resolved 23 km/s = +0.218 dex; sample residual would go -0.296 -> -0.514 dex if that correction were universal)

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
8. MUTATION CONTROLS -- does the de-selection machinery actually recover a known answer?
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    injected intrinsic offset +0.000 dex -> the pipeline would MEASURE -0.149 dex -> de-selection recovers -0.000 dex (error -0.000)
    injected intrinsic offset -0.300 dex -> the pipeline would MEASURE -0.377 dex -> de-selection recovers -0.300 dex (error +0.000)
  [PASS] M31b.a mutation: the section-4 de-selection is invertible.  Feed it a population that is exactly ON the BTFR and it returns zero; feed it one that is 0.30 dex below and it returns 0.30 dex below.  So the intrinsic offset quoted above is a measurement and not an artefact of the correction itself   (recovered -0.000 for a true 0.000, and -0.300 for a true -0.300)
  [PASS] M31b.b mutation: switch the selection OFF (drop the rms by 1000x so nothing is width-limited) and the manufactured bias vanishes -- confirming that the bias measured in section 4 is the width cut and not a bug in the mock   (with the detection limit removed the mock returns -0.0003 dex against -0.117 with it in place)
  [PASS] M31b.c mutation: the edge-on/face-on split of section 2 is not a random partition -- resampling 40 objects at random reproduces the sample median, not the split, so section 2's comparison carries information   (random 40-object medians centre on -0.299 against the full-sample -0.296)

======================================================================================================================
VERDICT -- ITEM 31 AFTER THE AUDIT: h31's LIABILITY IS WITHDRAWN AS A DIFFERENTIAL RESULT.  It was mostly selection.
======================================================================================================================
  h31 reported that the almost-darks sit -0.296 +- 0.028 dex below the framework's zero-parameter BTFR,
  -0.265 dex differentially against a mass-matched control, and called it a 9.5 sigma liability.  The
  differential does not survive this audit, and the reason is a selection effect h31 did not model.

  ALFALFA's sensitivity falls as W50^(-1/2), so at fixed HI mass and distance a FAST rotator is harder to detect
  than a slow one.  At fixed baryonic mass and inclination that is a cut on the BTFR residual itself.  It bites the
  almost-darks hard -- 50% of them would be undetectable if they sat exactly on the framework's BTFR -- and it
  barely touched h31's control, which was nearer (65 vs 79 Mpc) and brighter (SNR 9.4 vs 8.2).
  h31 compared a width-truncated sample with an untruncated one and read the difference as physics.

  Matched properly -- same baryonic mass, same HI mass, same inclination, same detection ceiling W_max, and with
  W_max built from flux and noise alone so that it is not a collider -- ordinary ALFALFA galaxies sit at
  -0.207 +- 0.018 dex and the almost-darks at -0.296.  The almost-dark property is worth
  -0.089 +- 0.033 dex (-2.7 sigma), not -0.265.  The forward model agrees independently: de-selecting
  the width cut self-consistently moves the absolute offset from -0.296 to -0.194 dex.

  What the audit could NOT break, and tried hard to:
   * the geometry.  Granting every galaxy the most face-on inclination its own measured axis ratio permits, for ANY
     oblate thickness, still leaves -0.287 dex.  Hubble's q0 = 0.2 is not doing the work.
   * the published counter-claim's mechanism (arXiv:2408.05269, low-inclination systematics).  The deficit is not
     carried by the face-on objects: the edge-on half sits at -0.325 dex against the face-on half's -0.182.
   * noise.  Symmetric axis-ratio error moves the median UP (+0.0001 dex) by Jensen's inequality, so scatter
     cannot manufacture a deficit; only a systematic factor-2 error in sin i could, and no geometry allows one.
  But the audit also failed its OWN model: the residual's trend with inclination (-0.205 dex from face-on to
  edge-on) is about three times steeper than the width selection can make (-0.073) and steeper than any constant
  offset (-0.055), and it is not axis-ratio noise (+0.0009).  An inclination-dependent systematic is loose in
  these measurements that nobody's model captures, so the sample median cannot be quoted to better than ~0.1 dex.

  So the RESIDUAL absolute offset of about -0.19 dex, shared by the almost-darks and by ordinary gas-rich
  galaxies of the same detectability, is not an inclination artefact.  Whether it is the framework's problem or a
  further layer of ALFALFA selection is NOT settled here, and this script does not claim it either way.

  Two bugs found, one in h31 and one in this audit's own first attempt:
   * h31: the control was matched on mass alone, so it was not width-truncated the way the almost-darks are.
   * here: matching the control on SNR instead looked decisive (-0.017 dex, effect gone) and was WRONG --
     SNR contains W50, so it conditions on the estimator's own numerator.  Its control had a median width of
     61 km/s against the mass-matched 113 km/s.  Reported, not quietly dropped.
   * and against interest a third time: on AGC 114905, the only object with resolved kinematics, this pipeline
     returns 13.9 km/s where interferometry gives 23 -- a +0.218 dex underestimate from the turbulence
     subtraction on a 27 km/s line.  The sample median width is 55 km/s where that correction is far smaller.

  STANDING: item 31 is a NULL, not a liability and not a win.  The almost-darks are NOT measurably off the BTFR
  relative to comparable ordinary galaxies (-0.09 +- 0.03 dex).  The item's Kepler-grade criterion -- 20+ objects
  on the line with <= 0.1 dex scatter -- is still not met, because the scatter is 0.26 dex, so this sample
  cannot measure a_0 without stellar mass-to-light either.  Nothing here favours one footing over the other.
  h31's headline number and its 9.5 sigma must NOT be quoted.  The decisive measurement remains resolved HI for
  more than one object, which is what the published dispute is also waiting on.

RESULT: 13 checks, 0 FAIL   rc=0
